For $x \ge 0$, $f(|x|) = f(x)$; reflecting the right half in the $y$-axis gives $y = f(|x|)$, which has $x$-intercepts $\pm 6$, $y$-intercept $-3$ and minimum points $(\pm 2, -4)$.

Taking the absolute value reflects the parts below the $x$-axis upwards, so $y = |f(|x|)|$:
- is even (symmetric about the $y$-axis);
- touches the $x$-axis at $x = \pm 6$ with sharp corners there;
- has a sharp point (local minimum) at $(0, 3)$;
- has local maxima at $(-2, 4)$ and $(2, 4)$;
- increases without bound for $|x| > 6$.
